        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "p_A = 150(0.4) = 60 and p_B = 50(0.6) = 30, so p_total = 90. Then y_A = 60/90 = 0.667."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u05_t01_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A: p_A = p_A^0 x_A (II). B: y_A = p_A/p_total (IV). C: p_total = p_A^0 + (p_B^0 - p_A^0)x_B (III). D: 1/p_total = y_A/p_A^0 + y_B/p_B^0 (I)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u05_t01_5",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        3
      ],
      "blind_answer": 3,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "A is true: a non-volatile solute lowers the solvent's vapour pressure. B is false: vapour pressure does not depend on the amount of liquid. C is true: the vapour is richer in the more volatile component. D is true: Raoult's law is the special case of Henry's law with K_H = p^0."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u05_t02_1",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        2
      ],
      "blind_answer": 2,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "n-Hexane and n-heptane are structurally similar non-polar alkanes. Their A–B interactions are about equal to their A–A and B–B interactions, so the mixture is nearly ideal (ΔH≈0, ΔV≈0)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u05_t02_2",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        1
      ],
      "blind_answer": 1,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Chloroform and acetone form a C–H···O hydrogen bond. Attraction between unlike molecules increases, giving negative deviation from Raoult's law: heat is released (ΔH<0) and the volume contracts (ΔV<0)."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u05_t02_3",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Ethanol–water shows positive deviation and forms a minimum boiling azeotrope (about 95% ethanol). HNO3–water and phenol–aniline show negative deviation, and benzene–toluene is nearly ideal."
    },
    {
      "q_id": "neet_che_u05_t02_4",
      "published": true,
      "reason": null,
      "manual_approved": false,
      "our_answer": [
        4
      ],
      "blind_answer": 4,
      "confidence": "high",
      "blind_working": "Ethanol–water actually shows positive deviation and forms a minimum boiling azeotrope, so A is false. R is true: ethanol does break some of the hydrogen bonds between water molecules."
